---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import TableOfContents from '../../components/TableOfContents.astro';
const breadcrumbs = [{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Setup', href: '/setup' }, { label: 'ROMs' }];
---

<Layout title="ROMs and system software" description="Find out which firmware and system files your emulator needs, where to obtain them, and how to check the configuration for your lesson." breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}>
  <article class="page">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">Setup Guide · ROMs and system software</p>
      <h1>Which files does your emulator need?</h1>
      <p class="lead">Check your lesson’s machine model and your emulator’s supplied files
        before downloading or buying anything. The requirements differ between systems,
        and your installation may already contain what you need.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="reading-layout">
      <div class="reading-body">
        <section aria-labelledby="meaning">
          <h2 id="meaning">A ROM image is a copy of stored data</h2>
          <p><strong>ROM</strong> means read-only memory. A system ROM holds built-in software,
            often called <strong>firmware</strong>. A ROM image is a file containing that data
            for an emulator to use. Original hardware normally already has its firmware;
            setting up an emulator is a separate task.</p>
          <p>The same word also appears in “game ROM”: a cartridge image containing a game’s
            program and data. A <code>.nes</code> file you build in the{' '}
            <a href="/systems/nintendo-entertainment-system">NES track</a> is your cartridge
            image. It is not a missing operating system you need to buy.</p>
          <p>Disk images are different again. On the Amiga, <strong>Kickstart</strong> is the
            ROM-based part of the operating system; <strong>Workbench</strong> includes the
            desktop and disk-based system software. They are separate files with version
            requirements. See <a href="https://www.amigaforever.com/kb/13-114">Cloanto’s ROM and operating-system file guide</a>.</p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="requirements">
          <h2 id="requirements">Start with your system</h2>
          <table>
            <caption>Firmware and media for the initial curriculum systems</caption>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">System</th><th scope="col">What to check</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row"><a href="/systems/sinclair-zx-spectrum/getting-started">ZX Spectrum</a></th>
                <td>Use the ROM set for the selected model. A 48K Spectrum and a 128K Spectrum
                  use different sets. Check what your Fuse package supplies; its{' '}
                  <a href="https://fuse-emulator.sourceforge.net/man/html/fuse.html">manual lists the ROM files and settings</a>.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row"><a href="/systems/commodore-64/getting-started">Commodore 64</a></th>
                <td>BASIC, KERNAL and character ROMs. Emulated disk drives may also need their
                  own ROMs. Check the installed package and the{' '}
                  <a href="https://vice-emu.sourceforge.io/vice_3.html">VICE system-file guide</a>.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row"><a href="/systems/commodore-amiga/getting-started">Commodore Amiga</a></th>
                <td>Kickstart matched to the Amiga model. Add Workbench disks or a configured
                  hard-disk image when the chosen programming environment needs them.
                  A lesson that boots its own game disk does not necessarily need a Workbench installation.</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row"><a href="/systems/nintendo-entertainment-system/getting-started">NES</a></th>
                <td>The standard cartridge-based lessons load your built <code>.nes</code> file
                  directly. They do not require a separate system ROM. This does not describe
                  add-ons such as the <a href="https://fceux.com/web/help/FamicomDiskSystem.html">Famicom Disk System</a>.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p>A missing-file message does not automatically mean you need a purchase. First
            check that you installed the emulator’s required data package and selected the
            correct model. Follow the documentation for that particular distribution.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="commodore-64" aria-labelledby="c64">
          <h2 id="c64">C64: identify the files before replacing them</h2>
          <p>VICE documents three C64 system images: <code>basic</code> (8 KiB),{' '}
            <code>kernal</code> (8 KiB) and <code>chargen</code> (4 KiB). The character ROM
            supplies character shapes. Disk-drive firmware belongs to the selected drive,
            not the C64’s main ROM set.</p>
          <p>If VICE reports a missing ROM, check its system-file search locations or set an
            explicit file path in its ROM settings. File size is a useful check, but does
            not prove that a file is the right revision. The{' '}
            <a href="https://vice-emu.sourceforge.io/vice_3.html">VICE manual</a> describes
            the files, paths and drive requirements.</p>
          <p><a href="https://www.c64forever.com/">C64 Forever</a>, published by Cloanto, is
            one commercial source of licensed Commodore system software. Compare the{' '}
            <a href="https://www.c64forever.com/editions/">current editions</a> and their
            terms before buying. If you intend to use the files in another emulator,
            confirm that the edition provides access to the files and permits that use.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="commodore-amiga" aria-labelledby="amiga">
          <h2 id="amiga">Amiga: match Kickstart to the environment</h2>
          <p>Choose the model and Kickstart revision specified by the lesson. A newer ROM
            is not automatically a better match. Assembly games,{' '}
            <a href="/systems/commodore-amiga/amos/getting-started">AMOS</a> and{' '}
            <a href="/systems/commodore-amiga/blitz/getting-started">Blitz BASIC</a> can use
            different configurations; follow the relevant setup guide.</p>
          <p><a href="https://www.amigaforever.com/">Amiga Forever</a>, also published by
            Cloanto, supplies licensed Kickstart and disk-based system files. Its Value
            edition covers early versions; Plus supplies a broader range. Check the{' '}
            <a href="https://www.amigaforever.com/kb/13-114">file list</a> against your
            required version and model, and consult the supplier for current prices.</p>
          <p>The downloadable desktop installer is for Windows. On macOS or Linux, read
            the supplier’s <a href="https://www.amigaforever.com/diversity/">cross-platform guidance</a>{' '}
            before buying: using the files with another emulator is a manual setup, and you
            need an accessible distribution format. Do not assume the Windows installer will run on your host.</p>
          <ol>
            <li>Choose a way to access the distribution. For macOS or Linux, Cloanto
              describes a platform-neutral DVD image made by Plus on Windows, or the
              physical DVD supplied with Premium. Mount that image or disc on your host
              to access its files. See the <a href="https://www.amigaforever.com/gnulinux/">cross-platform distribution options</a>;
              creating the Plus image requires access to Windows.</li>
            <li>Locate the supplied ROM and system files. In Amiga Forever, the File menu’s
              Open Folder / Amiga Files command leads to the shared files; ROMs are under{' '}
              <code>Amiga Files/Shared/rom</code>. The{' '}
              <a href="https://www.amigaforever.com/kb/13-114">supplier’s instructions</a>{' '}
              also describe distribution-media access.</li>
            <li>Import or select the matching Kickstart in your emulator. For FS-UAE, follow
              its <a href="https://fs-uae.net/docs/getting-started/">file-placement instructions</a>.
              Keep a supplied <code>rom.key</code> with encrypted ROM files when those instructions require it.</li>
            <li>Attach the lesson’s disk image, or prepare the Workbench environment required
              by its language guide. A <code>.adf</code> disk image does not go in the Kickstart ROM setting.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="alternatives">
          <h2 id="alternatives">Hardware extraction and replacement firmware</h2>
          <p>It is technically possible to read firmware from original hardware. For Amiga,
            the <a href="https://fs-uae.net/docs/kickstart-roms/">FS-UAE guide</a> names
            extraction tools such as Transrom and Grabkick. Extraction requires a working
            machine and a way to transfer the file; it is not just a download step.</p>
          <p>Check the applicable licence and local rules before copying or using an extracted
            image. Owning hardware alone is not a blanket permission to redistribute its
            software. Cloanto publishes its <a href="https://www.amigaforever.com/kb/13-122">position on ROM use and distribution</a>;
            that supplier statement should not be mistaken for a complete account of every jurisdiction’s law.</p>
          <p><a href="https://github.com/MEGA65/open-roms">Open ROMs</a> develops replacement
            firmware for Commodore machines. Read its{' '}
            <a href="https://github.com/MEGA65/open-roms/blob/master/STATUS.md">implementation status</a>{' '}
            and licence before choosing it. Being written in assembly does not by itself
            make a program independent of the original ROM’s behaviour.</p>
          <p>FS-UAE also documents an <a href="https://fs-uae.net/docs/kickstart-roms/">AROS-based replacement</a>,
            with compatibility differences and different selection behaviour in FS-UAE and
            its Launcher. These alternatives are useful subjects for investigation, but this
            guide does not establish that a whole Code198x track works with either replacement.</p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="checking">
          <h2 id="checking">Check the configuration before starting a lesson</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Record the selection.</strong> Note the emulator version, machine model,
              RAM configuration and firmware revision. Use the emulator’s recognised ROM
              identity or a documented checksum where available; a filename can be changed.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the boot.</strong> For a C64 BASIC environment, look for its startup
              screen and <code>READY.</code> prompt. For a 48K Spectrum, compare the startup
              screen with <a href="/systems/sinclair-zx-spectrum/basic/meet-basic/unit-01">the first BASIC lesson</a>. For an Amiga without a disk, expect the
              selected Kickstart’s disk request, not a Workbench desktop.</li>
            <li><strong>Run the lesson’s example.</strong> Load the supplied or built program and
              compare its screen and controls with the lesson. For NES, this is the first
              useful check; there is no separate desktop to reach.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>A successful boot checks only part of the setup. If a later example differs,
            investigate the program, emulator, model and firmware rather than assuming that
            every ROM or emulator version behaves identically.</p>
          <p>Code198x does not supply commercial system ROMs in its lesson downloads. Keep
            their licences separate from <a href="/standards#licence">our prose licence</a>{' '}
            and the code samples’ terms. For help, report the page, configuration and exact
            error through the <a href="/setup#help">Setup Guide</a>; do not attach ROM files.</p>
        </section>
      </div>
      <aside><TableOfContents selector=".reading-body" /></aside>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page { max-width: var(--container-max-width); margin: 0 auto; padding: var(--space-10) var(--gutter) var(--space-16); }
  header { margin-bottom: var(--space-10); }
  .eyebrow { font-family: var(--font-family-sans); font-size: var(--h-text-small); color: var(--h-ink-muted); }
  h1 { font-family: var(--font-family-read); font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 5vw, 3.6rem); line-height: 1.08; color: var(--h-ink); margin: var(--space-3) 0 var(--space-6); }
  .lead { font-size: 1.15rem; }
  .reading-layout { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 240px; gap: var(--space-12); align-items: start; }
  .reading-body { min-width: 0; }
  aside { position: sticky; top: 7rem; }
  section { margin-top: var(--space-10); scroll-margin-top: 7rem; }
  section:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
  h2, h3 { font-family: var(--font-family-sans); color: var(--h-ink); line-height: 1.3; }
  h2 { font-size: 1.4rem; } h3 { font-size: 1.1rem; margin-top: 0; }
  p, li { font-family: var(--font-family-read); max-width: 70ch; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--h-ink-body); }
  p { margin: var(--space-4) 0; }
  li { margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  ul, ol { padding-left: var(--space-6); }
  a { color: inherit; text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: .15em; }

  .cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: var(--space-5); }
  .card { border: 1px solid var(--color-border); background: var(--color-surface); padding: var(--space-5); }
  .card p { font-size: var(--h-text-small); margin-bottom: 0; }
  @media (max-width: 1100px) { .reading-layout { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } aside { display: none; } }
  @media (max-width: 680px) { .cards { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }

  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: var(--space-5) 0; font-size: var(--h-text-small); }
  caption { text-align: left; color: var(--h-ink-muted); margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  th, td { padding: var(--space-4); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); text-align: left; vertical-align: top; line-height: 1.6; }
  th { background: var(--color-surface); }
  td { color: var(--h-ink-body); }
  @media (max-width: 600px) { th, td { padding: var(--space-2); } th[scope="row"] { width: 28%; overflow-wrap: anywhere; } }
</style>
